---
/**
 * /privacy — privacy policy.
 *
 * Written against what the code actually does, not from a template. Every
 * claim here was checked against the source: the third-party list is the
 * complete set of external requests the site makes, and the Velocity Profile
 * section matches the record shape in src/lib/velocity-profile/storage.ts.
 *
 * If you add a tracking pixel, an embedded map, a chat widget, a font CDN, or
 * a new place form data lands, this page is wrong until you update it. The
 * same goes for changing what the quiz stores.
 *
 * Deliberately a static page rather than Keystatic content: it changes rarely,
 * and precision matters more than editing convenience.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const updated = 'November 23, 2026';

const eyebrow = 'font-mono text-xs text-muted uppercase tracking-widest';
const h2 = 'font-display leading-display text-headline text-3xl mt-12 mb-4';
const h3 = 'text-xl text-headline mt-8 mb-3';
const p = 'text-base text-body leading-body';
const link =
  'font-light underline decoration-accent decoration-2 underline-offset-4 hover:text-headline transition duration-fast';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="What interactivism.com collects, why, and what you can do about it."
>
  <div class="px-6 lg:px-12 py-16 lg:py-24 max-w-content mx-auto">

    <p class={eyebrow}>Last updated {updated}</p>
    <h1 class="font-display leading-display text-headline mt-4 mb-6">Privacy</h1>

    <div class="max-w-prose">
      <p class="text-lg text-body leading-body">
        This covers interactivism.com. It is written in plain language, because
        a policy nobody can read is not much of a policy.
      </p>

      <!-- ── Short version ──────────────────────────────────── -->
      <div class="bg-surface-alt rounded-sm px-6 py-5 mt-8">
        <p class={`${eyebrow} mb-4`}>The short version</p>
        <ul class="space-y-2">
          <li class={p}>We do not sell your information, and we do not share it with advertisers.</li>
          <li class={p}>We collect what you hand us — a form, the assessment — plus standard web analytics.</li>
          <li class={p}>You can ask us what we hold about you, or ask us to delete it, and we will.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- ── Who we are ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <h2 class={h2}>Who we are</h2>
      <p class={p}>
        Interactivism is a product design and development studio at
        40 E. Colorado Blvd, Suite A, Pasadena, CA 91105. For anything on this
        page, write to
        <a href="mailto:hello@interactivism.com" class={link}>hello@interactivism.com</a>.
      </p>

      <!-- ── What we collect ────────────────────────────────── -->
      <h2 class={h2}>What we collect, and why</h2>

      <h3 class={h3}>When you browse the site</h3>
      <p class={p}>
        Google Analytics records that a page was viewed, roughly where in the
        world the request came from, and what kind of device and browser you
        are using. It sets cookies to recognize a returning browser. We use it
        to see which work and which writing people actually read.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        Our web host keeps standard server logs, which include IP addresses, as
        part of running and securing the site.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        Fonts are served from this site rather than a font CDN, so no third
        party sees your visit just because a page loaded a typeface. Two case
        studies embed video through YouTube’s no-cookie domain, which does not
        set tracking cookies unless you press play. The map on the contact page
        is a static image, not an embedded map, so Google is not called when
        you open it.
      </p>

      <h3 class={h3}>When you use the contact form</h3>
      <p class={p}>
        We get your name, email address, company if you fill it in, and your
        message. It goes to our inbox through our host’s form handling. We use
        it to reply to you.
      </p>

      <h3 class={h3}>When you take the Product Velocity Profile</h3>
      <p class={p}>
        The assessment stores your answers to the twelve questions, the result
        we calculate from them, your email address, and the name, company,
        level, function, and industry you give us after the questions. It also
        records how you arrived — the campaign tag in the link you clicked, or
        the site that referred you — and the time you submitted.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        We use it to send you your report and, if the conversation looks
        relevant to both of us, to follow up. Two specifics worth stating:
        <strong class="text-headline font-medium">we do not store your IP address with your answers</strong>,
        and the link to your report online contains a random token rather than
        anything about you, so the address itself gives nothing away. Anyone you
        forward that link to can read the report, which is deliberate.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        Some people are invited to the assessment by a link that skips the
        email step. If that is you, we store your answers, the result, and a
        tag naming the invitation the link belonged to — one tag shared by
        everyone who got that invitation, not one per person — and
        <strong class="text-headline font-medium">no email address, name, or company</strong>.
        We cannot tell who you are from it. Your report then offers a “Send my
        result” link, which opens a message in your own mail app containing
        your profile and the link to your report. Nothing goes to us unless you
        press send; if you do, we can read the report and we know it is yours.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        To keep the form from being abused we briefly keep a one-way scrambled
        version of your IP address, in a separate place that is never connected
        to a submission.
      </p>

      <h3 class={h3}>When you book a call</h3>
      <p class={p}>
        “Book a call” hands you to Google’s appointment scheduling. What you
        enter there is covered by
        <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noopener" class={link}>Google’s privacy policy</a>,
        not this one. We see the booking.
      </p>

      <!-- ── Third parties ──────────────────────────────────── -->
      <h2 class={h2}>Who else touches it</h2>
      <p class={p}>
        These are the only companies that handle information from this site.
        They act on our instructions; none of them get it to use for their own
        marketing.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-6">
        <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-subtle">
              <th class={`${eyebrow} py-3 pr-4 font-normal`}>Who</th>
              <th class={`${eyebrow} py-3 font-normal`}>What for</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {[
              ['Netlify', 'Hosting the site, handling contact form submissions, and storing assessment results'],
              ['Google Analytics', 'Understanding how the site is used'],
              ['Resend', 'Delivering the assessment report and our internal notification'],
              ['Google', 'Appointment scheduling, when you book a call'],
            ].map(([who, what]) => (
              <tr class="border-b border-subtle align-top">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-base text-headline whitespace-nowrap">{who}</td>
                <td class={`py-3 ${p}`}>{what}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <!-- ── Cookies ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <h2 class={h2}>Cookies and similar storage</h2>
      <p class={p}>
        Google Analytics sets cookies to tell one browser from another and one
        visit from another. That is the only cookie set on the public site.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        While you are taking the assessment, your browser also holds the
        campaign tag from the link you arrived through, so it can be submitted
        with your answers. It is cleared when you close the tab.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        You can block or clear cookies in your browser, or install
        <a
          href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout"
          rel="noopener"
          class={link}
        >Google’s opt-out extension</a>, and the site will work exactly the same.
      </p>

      <!-- ── Retention ──────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <h2 class={h2}>How long we keep it</h2>
      <p class={p}>
        Honestly: we do not delete this on a schedule. Contact messages stay in
        our email, and assessment results stay in our storage, until we have no
        use for them or you ask us to remove them. Google Analytics data expires
        on Google’s own schedule.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        If you would rather we did not hold on to yours, say so and we will
        delete it.
      </p>

      <!-- ── Your choices ───────────────────────────────────── -->
      <h2 class={h2}>What you can ask for</h2>
      <p class={p}>
        Write to <a href="mailto:hello@interactivism.com" class={link}>hello@interactivism.com</a>
        and we will tell you what we hold about you, correct it, or delete it.
        We offer this to everyone who asks, wherever you live, rather than only
        where a law requires it. We will not make you jump through hoops, and
        we will not charge you.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        If you are in the EU or UK and are not satisfied with how we handled a
        request, you can complain to your national data protection authority.
      </p>

      <!-- ── Children ───────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <h2 class={h2}>Children</h2>
      <p class={p}>
        This is a business site and nothing here is aimed at children. We do not
        knowingly collect information from anyone under 16.
      </p>

      <!-- ── Changes ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <h2 class={h2}>Changes</h2>
      <p class={p}>
        If we start collecting something new, we will change this page and move
        the date at the top. There is no archive of old versions; if that
        matters to you, keep a copy.
      </p>

    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
